WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:04.206
- [Chris] Let's do it.
- Welcome everybody,

00:00:04.206 --> 00:00:06.734
another YouTube Hangout,
super pumped to be here.

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:08.153
I've got a new friend with me.

00:00:08.153 --> 00:00:12.190
Chris runs a podcast called
The Entrepreneur Hour,

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.131
and he wanted to interview
me for his podcast

00:00:14.131 --> 00:00:16.354
so I said yes, let's do it.

00:00:16.354 --> 00:00:17.834
But how about we also
make it a YouTube video,

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:20.830
so I could expose him and
his ideas to you guys?

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.904
So before getting into the questions

00:00:22.904 --> 00:00:25.705
and Chris will run into
his intro for his show,

00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:27.881
Chris, welcome aboard
man, how are you doing?

00:00:27.881 --> 00:00:29.121
- Hey man, thanks for having me.

00:00:29.121 --> 00:00:30.040
Good to be here.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.993
- Quickly, for my audience,
just give a little background,

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:34.847
what you do, what The
Entrepreneur Hour's about,

00:00:34.847 --> 00:00:36.696
and where they can find you.

00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:38.837
- Totally. So, started my first business

00:00:38.837 --> 00:00:40.737
from my college apartment,
actually my brother and I,

00:00:40.737 --> 00:00:44.062
and had really no intention
for it to do anything

00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:46.536
other than just to pay
our bills and our college.

00:00:46.536 --> 00:00:48.807
I kind of always was entrepreneurial,

00:00:48.807 --> 00:00:50.315
starting businesses when
I was in high school,

00:00:50.315 --> 00:00:51.924
even back to middle
school, just lawn care,

00:00:51.924 --> 00:00:53.083
and, you know, just stuff that's

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.344
outside of the hour for pay model.

00:00:55.344 --> 00:00:57.271
Lo and behold, we got traction,

00:00:57.271 --> 00:00:59.538
unexpectedly got traction
with that business in college.

00:00:59.538 --> 00:01:01.645
We found ourselves scaling
to 1.2 million dollars

00:01:01.645 --> 00:01:03.523
within 36 months with that business,

00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:05.980
doing operations in 32
states around the country,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.336
working with five of the seven major

00:01:07.336 --> 00:01:10.456
furniture turnkey providers.

00:01:10.456 --> 00:01:13.227
So I was having all these
incredible conversations

00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:15.588
with people, other
entrepreneurs and mentors,

00:01:15.588 --> 00:01:17.513
things of that nature, and I just said,

00:01:17.513 --> 00:01:19.176
"Man, these conversations are so powerful

00:01:19.176 --> 00:01:21.553
"and they're so impactful to me.

00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:24.052
"Why aren't we recording
this and paying it forward

00:01:24.052 --> 00:01:25.811
"and giving back to the
entrepreneurial community?"

00:01:25.811 --> 00:01:27.777
A lot of things that, you
know I always said this,

00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:30.034
but if I had known x six months earlier

00:01:30.034 --> 00:01:31.079
than when I actually learned it,

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:32.592
maybe my journey would have
been a little bit easier

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:35.065
because truth be told,

00:01:35.065 --> 00:01:36.931
a lot of people think
scaling is a lot of fun.

00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:39.176
And while it is, it's certainly
it's very, very difficult

00:01:39.176 --> 00:01:40.892
to scale at that kind of a rapid rate

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.416
in a matter of 36 months.

00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:44.393
So, that's kind of how
the show came to fruition.

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:46.630
As you can tell, I got
all my podcasting gear

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.328
all set up in here, so I look
like a true podcaster, right?

00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:51.255
So, lo and behold, man,

00:01:51.255 --> 00:01:53.778
within two weeks of launching the podcast,

00:01:53.778 --> 00:01:55.596
we were top five new and noteworthy

00:01:55.596 --> 00:01:58.067
under business, health, and education,

00:01:58.067 --> 00:01:59.688
and we parlayed that
with some major guests.

00:01:59.688 --> 00:02:01.863
We went from, like, 30 downloads a day

00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:03.708
when we first launched
in the first couple days

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:05.382
to, like, thousands a day,

00:02:05.382 --> 00:02:07.273
and it just has been just a crazy ride

00:02:07.273 --> 00:02:08.744
but I think it's been exciting.

00:02:08.744 --> 00:02:09.805
It's been exciting to hear

00:02:09.805 --> 00:02:10.938
from the entrepreneurial community

00:02:10.938 --> 00:02:12.532
and know that they're getting
value out of the show.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.878
And then, you know,
last week I interviewed

00:02:13.878 --> 00:02:15.031
Daymond John from Shark Tank,

00:02:15.031 --> 00:02:16.859
and so, it's been awesome
for me to connect with those

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:19.393
just incredible entrepreneurs,
people like yourself

00:02:19.393 --> 00:02:21.333
that otherwise I wouldn't
have had really an opportunity

00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:22.548
to connect with or
maybe just wouldn't have

00:02:22.548 --> 00:02:24.385
come to fruition or would have taken me

00:02:24.385 --> 00:02:27.317
months and months and months
to find the medium to connect.

00:02:27.317 --> 00:02:29.956
So, that's kind of my story in a nutshell.

00:02:29.956 --> 00:02:31.339
I'm also now involved
with several endeavors

00:02:31.339 --> 00:02:34.271
here where I'm based in the Atlanta area.

00:02:34.271 --> 00:02:35.216
- I love it, man.

00:02:35.216 --> 00:02:36.148
Well, thanks for the intro,

00:02:36.148 --> 00:02:37.376
and turn it over to you,

00:02:37.376 --> 00:02:40.031
whatever you need to do for
your podcast, here we go.

00:02:40.031 --> 00:02:40.864
- Let's do it.

00:02:40.864 --> 00:02:43.339
So, I want to flip the
question on you then.

00:02:43.339 --> 00:02:44.669
So, tell me about your journey

00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:46.576
and tell me about major life events,

00:02:46.576 --> 00:02:49.712
things that kind of led
to what you're doing now.

00:02:49.712 --> 00:02:51.560
- So I can give you a high level,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.780
and then, if you want to dig
deeper into any of those things,

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.635
let me know.

00:02:54.635 --> 00:02:55.861
I'm happier to go further.

00:02:55.861 --> 00:02:59.102
Growing up, I never thought I
was going to be an entrepreneur.

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:01.132
I had a lot of entrepreneurial tendencies,

00:03:01.132 --> 00:03:03.597
selling art, selling baseball cards,

00:03:03.597 --> 00:03:04.483
a bunch of different things,

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:06.424
but I didn't have any
entrepreneurs in my family

00:03:06.424 --> 00:03:07.925
or immediate kind of community,

00:03:07.925 --> 00:03:09.640
so I thought I wanted to be a banker.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.930
I feel like in my high school yearbook,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.176
where are you going to be in 10 years,

00:03:13.176 --> 00:03:16.048
a VP at a bank, it's where I
thought my tracks was going to be.

00:03:16.048 --> 00:03:19.232
I go to university, and I'm
on that path to be a banker.

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.574
Connect with two
entrepreneurs who need help,

00:03:21.574 --> 00:03:25.416
and I join their business
as one of the owners.

00:03:25.416 --> 00:03:28.997
And we struggled a lot making $300 a month

00:03:28.997 --> 00:03:31.247
and barely able to survive.

00:03:32.644 --> 00:03:35.239
I get opportunities to get
those dream jobs at banks,

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:38.323
where I'm earning 80 to 100k a year,

00:03:38.323 --> 00:03:41.057
and I decide not to do
those and do this business

00:03:41.057 --> 00:03:42.668
that is not making money,

00:03:42.668 --> 00:03:44.485
the hardest decision of my life.

00:03:44.485 --> 00:03:48.652
And then from there, end up
sucking some more at my company.

00:03:50.792 --> 00:03:53.474
Finally find a turnaround point,

00:03:53.474 --> 00:03:55.851
and a couple years later, we get acquired.

00:03:55.851 --> 00:03:57.549
We can go through that if you want.

00:03:57.549 --> 00:03:59.549
From there, I became a venture capitalist,

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:00.832
raise half a million to 15 million

00:04:00.832 --> 00:04:02.766
for companies wanting to expand,

00:04:02.766 --> 00:04:05.448
and now, started helping entrepreneurs

00:04:05.448 --> 00:04:08.929
and had a popular website,
have a popular YouTube channel,

00:04:08.929 --> 00:04:10.940
have a book with Penguin Random House

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.291
and my goal is to help
a billion entrepreneurs

00:04:13.291 --> 00:04:15.320
and that's what fuels me daily.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:16.436
- Yeah, and I really want to get into that

00:04:16.436 --> 00:04:18.126
'cause I think that's awesome
that you have that mission.

00:04:18.126 --> 00:04:19.135
And I want to talk to you,

00:04:19.135 --> 00:04:20.903
it seems like everything you've done

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:22.661
as far as your branding is concerned

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:25.861
is like your power statement of believe.

00:04:25.861 --> 00:04:29.733
So, kind of talk about what that
power statement means to you

00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:31.355
and then how important
it is for entrepreneurs

00:04:31.355 --> 00:04:33.593
to have that positive mindset.

00:04:33.593 --> 00:04:37.076
- So, I believe everybody
has one word inside them.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:39.600
They have one core value
that makes up who they are.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.750
It's the topic of my book that came out.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.505
For me, it's believe,
and it's more than just

00:04:45.505 --> 00:04:46.869
a marketing thing.

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:49.152
I think a lot of people
see, that's great branding,

00:04:49.152 --> 00:04:49.985
that's great marketing.

00:04:49.985 --> 00:04:50.818
- No, totally, yeah.

00:04:50.818 --> 00:04:51.980
- And it is, it is.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.877
It's great marketing,
but it's authentic to me.

00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:56.201
It's who I am, it's what I do.

00:04:56.201 --> 00:04:59.430
Belief for me comes from my parents.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.451
It's the reason why I have a picture of me

00:05:01.451 --> 00:05:04.620
at seven years old up on my
wall with my parents behind me.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.165
They would always teach me that
I was Castrilli Carmichael,

00:05:07.165 --> 00:05:08.558
I could do anything that I wanted.

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:10.962
And so, whenever I
didn't do well at school

00:05:10.962 --> 00:05:13.392
or whatever it was, got other problems,

00:05:13.392 --> 00:05:15.838
the answer would always be,
"You're a Castrilli Carmichael.

00:05:15.838 --> 00:05:16.770
"You will get through this.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.181
"You can do anything that you want."

00:05:18.181 --> 00:05:20.662
And so, believe a lot came from them.

00:05:20.662 --> 00:05:22.700
And I think it's important to understand

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.034
what you do stand for and
then start to live that life,

00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:27.341
and for the entrepreneurs out there,

00:05:27.341 --> 00:05:29.448
bring that to your business as well.

00:05:29.448 --> 00:05:31.756
And so, understanding
that I was about believe

00:05:31.756 --> 00:05:33.558
and that was my most important core value,

00:05:33.558 --> 00:05:36.654
I didn't really know that
until later on in life,

00:05:36.654 --> 00:05:39.502
but that now allowed me
to get a turbo charge

00:05:39.502 --> 00:05:41.369
into my business, grow a lot faster,

00:05:41.369 --> 00:05:44.887
make better content, connect
with people on a higher level,

00:05:44.887 --> 00:05:47.481
and also have a lot more
fun, make a lot more money,

00:05:47.481 --> 00:05:49.683
feel like I'm doing purpose-driven work

00:05:49.683 --> 00:05:52.645
and that allows me to go
out and have a big impact.

00:05:52.645 --> 00:05:55.476
- Yeah, so when did you
find the platform of

00:05:55.476 --> 00:05:58.109
creating content, doing video,
and things of that nature?

00:05:58.109 --> 00:06:00.173
Did you just kind of
stumble upon doing that?

00:06:00.173 --> 00:06:02.263
- It started actually on my website,

00:06:02.263 --> 00:06:05.605
so the thing that saved
my company early on

00:06:05.605 --> 00:06:07.702
was modeling success.

00:06:07.702 --> 00:06:09.221
When I realized

00:06:09.221 --> 00:06:11.031
I'm not the first guy to
try to do this before,

00:06:11.031 --> 00:06:13.148
somebody's already done
it, let me figure out who

00:06:13.148 --> 00:06:14.237
and model their success.

00:06:14.237 --> 00:06:16.448
That saved from going from $300 a month

00:06:16.448 --> 00:06:20.443
to actually having a paycheck
and building a real business.

00:06:20.443 --> 00:06:23.396
And so, I modeled Bill
Gates, how he started,

00:06:23.396 --> 00:06:25.537
zero to one, I wanted to learn that.

00:06:25.537 --> 00:06:29.768
And so, I then built a website
to try to help entrepreneurs

00:06:29.768 --> 00:06:32.208
avoid some of the struggles that I faced

00:06:32.208 --> 00:06:33.506
early on in my career,

00:06:33.506 --> 00:06:35.674
and I shared the stories of
all these famous entrepreneurs,

00:06:35.674 --> 00:06:37.502
hoping that people could learn from them.

00:06:37.502 --> 00:06:40.568
I was learning myself and others
could learn from them too,

00:06:40.568 --> 00:06:43.248
hopefully avoid their pitfalls
and learn from their success.

00:06:43.248 --> 00:06:45.548
And that evolved into a YouTube channel

00:06:45.548 --> 00:06:48.906
just because I'm much
more of a visual learner.

00:06:48.906 --> 00:06:52.505
I would rather see something than read it.

00:06:52.505 --> 00:06:56.172
And so, if I could read
Steve Jobs's speech,

00:06:57.353 --> 00:07:00.334
that's great, but if I
could see him delivering it,

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:02.441
it just touched me more deeply.

00:07:02.441 --> 00:07:03.736
I resonated with it more.

00:07:03.736 --> 00:07:05.689
I was able to understand it more.

00:07:05.689 --> 00:07:07.905
And so, it evolved to a YouTube channel

00:07:07.905 --> 00:07:09.720
because it's much more of a visual medium.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.182
So it started selfishly for me

00:07:11.182 --> 00:07:13.576
to learn more about these
famous entrepreneurs,

00:07:13.576 --> 00:07:15.997
to continue my, to build my growth,

00:07:15.997 --> 00:07:18.811
and I shared it with
others and thankfully,

00:07:18.811 --> 00:07:20.648
people have been inspired as well by it

00:07:20.648 --> 00:07:22.500
and continue to come back.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:23.661
- Well, I think it's really interesting

00:07:23.661 --> 00:07:25.446
because you talked about
how believe is unique

00:07:25.446 --> 00:07:26.851
and authentic to you and how the video.

00:07:26.851 --> 00:07:27.945
So, you've created things that really were

00:07:27.945 --> 00:07:30.490
authentic and unique to you,
and that's really what's

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.041
made your brand take off, it sounds like.

00:07:33.041 --> 00:07:34.746
So that's really, really fascinating,

00:07:34.746 --> 00:07:37.447
just make it, just be your
best unique self, you know.

00:07:37.447 --> 00:07:39.842
- Yeah, and when I first found believe,

00:07:39.842 --> 00:07:43.203
I thought it was too
bold, too big an idea,

00:07:43.203 --> 00:07:45.927
other people have done it before.

00:07:45.927 --> 00:07:48.715
Justin Bieber had his Believe tour,

00:07:48.715 --> 00:07:51.509
and Cher had a song Believe.
- I forgot about that, yeah.

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.899
- Shamu the whale has a show
at SeaWorld called Believe.

00:07:54.899 --> 00:07:58.434
It's been used, and I
think what happens a lot,

00:07:58.434 --> 00:08:01.652
especially with entrepreneurs,
is we talk ourselves down

00:08:01.652 --> 00:08:03.093
from big ideas.

00:08:03.093 --> 00:08:05.905
We'll come up with a big
idea and say this is great,

00:08:05.905 --> 00:08:09.188
here's how we can go and
change the world, rah-rah.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:11.466
And then, the next day,
it's not even our friends

00:08:11.466 --> 00:08:13.803
and our family who put
us down, it's ourselves.

00:08:13.803 --> 00:08:15.471
We are our own worst enemy.

00:08:15.471 --> 00:08:16.736
The next day, you wake up and say,

00:08:16.736 --> 00:08:18.505
"That idea that we had, that's too big.

00:08:18.505 --> 00:08:19.338
"I can't do that.

00:08:19.338 --> 00:08:22.163
"I don't have the resources or
the capabilities to do that."

00:08:22.163 --> 00:08:24.949
And we talk ourselves down from
doing the big, bold things.

00:08:24.949 --> 00:08:26.748
And so, I didn't want to not do it

00:08:26.748 --> 00:08:29.660
because I was afraid of
doing something bold.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.808
I think that happens way
too often for entrepreneurs.

00:08:32.808 --> 00:08:33.700
- Now, you talked about your parents.

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.005
Were they both entrepreneurial,

00:08:36.005 --> 00:08:38.478
entrepreneur driven, or kind
of how'd they feel about it

00:08:38.478 --> 00:08:40.562
when you started pursuing
entrepreneurship?

00:08:40.562 --> 00:08:44.260
- They are not entrepreneurial at all.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.672
My dad worked for the government,

00:08:45.672 --> 00:08:47.142
basically his whole life, and then retired

00:08:47.142 --> 00:08:49.065
and became a consultant.

00:08:49.065 --> 00:08:51.678
The thing that was great about my parents

00:08:51.678 --> 00:08:55.752
were they always encouraged
us to do whatever we wanted.

00:08:55.752 --> 00:08:57.871
And they made us feel like we
could do whatever we wanted.

00:08:57.871 --> 00:09:01.260
And so, they lived more traditional lives

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.739
and I think in part
because on my mom's side,

00:09:04.739 --> 00:09:08.018
her parents kind of grew
up a lot more strict

00:09:08.018 --> 00:09:09.790
and so, she didn't have the freedom to do

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:10.958
some of the things she wanted to do.

00:09:10.958 --> 00:09:13.145
And so, the result was
I'm going to give my kids

00:09:13.145 --> 00:09:15.039
lots of freedom and confidence and belief.

00:09:15.039 --> 00:09:17.382
And so, I have two
sisters. I'm in the middle.

00:09:17.382 --> 00:09:20.041
All three of us went totally different,

00:09:20.041 --> 00:09:22.478
wacky, creative paths.

00:09:22.478 --> 00:09:26.606
My older sister did a bunch
of Silicon Valley start-ups

00:09:26.606 --> 00:09:31.204
and is raising money and CEO
of a new company down there.

00:09:31.204 --> 00:09:33.912
My younger sister went
to Africa for 10 years

00:09:33.912 --> 00:09:36.026
and worked for the UN.
- Oh wow.

00:09:36.026 --> 00:09:38.399
- And is helping child
soldiers come out of

00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:42.631
the war zone and helping them
get back to a normal life.

00:09:42.631 --> 00:09:44.773
- That's awesome.
- And just crazy stuff.

00:09:44.773 --> 00:09:46.955
And so, none of us have led a traditional.

00:09:46.955 --> 00:09:50.572
None of us led the traditional
life that my parents led,

00:09:50.572 --> 00:09:53.767
and I attribute that to them
instilling that belief in us

00:09:53.767 --> 00:09:55.350
that we could do whatever we wanted.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:56.433
- Yeah, cool, man.

00:09:56.433 --> 00:09:58.026
Well, so you talked a little bit earlier,

00:09:58.026 --> 00:09:59.776
you talked about helping and inspiring

00:09:59.776 --> 00:10:01.082
one billion entrepreneurs,

00:10:01.082 --> 00:10:03.094
which is quite an ambitious

00:10:03.094 --> 00:10:05.625
feat you're setting out for yourself.

00:10:05.625 --> 00:10:08.979
But talk about what the
world would look like,

00:10:08.979 --> 00:10:11.626
this is very, kind of
ambiguous or vague question,

00:10:11.626 --> 00:10:14.047
but talk about what
that would actually mean

00:10:14.047 --> 00:10:17.199
socioeconomically to the world in general.

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:18.975
What kind of impact would that make,

00:10:18.975 --> 00:10:21.865
or as you see it or how
you envision it would?

00:10:21.865 --> 00:10:23.344
- I think all the world's problems

00:10:23.344 --> 00:10:25.175
will be solved by entrepreneurs.

00:10:25.175 --> 00:10:27.535
I think it's on us to save it.

00:10:27.535 --> 00:10:29.603
I don't think it's going
to be the government

00:10:29.603 --> 00:10:32.780
or who the president
is or big corporations.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:36.282
The innovation happens from entrepreneurs.

00:10:36.282 --> 00:10:41.112
And countries, wars are
stopped because of economics,

00:10:41.112 --> 00:10:42.915
because there's a lot
of trade that happens,

00:10:42.915 --> 00:10:44.929
because it's us doing the work.

00:10:44.929 --> 00:10:46.731
And so, I think every major problem,

00:10:46.731 --> 00:10:49.779
if you're looking at
healthcare and diseases

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:52.666
or if you're looking at space exploration

00:10:52.666 --> 00:10:55.557
or if you're looking at world
peace or whatever it is,

00:10:55.557 --> 00:10:57.633
I think it's going to be
solved by entrepreneurs.

00:10:57.633 --> 00:11:01.071
And I think a lot of people
have a genius level talent

00:11:01.071 --> 00:11:04.012
at something inside them that they're not

00:11:04.012 --> 00:11:07.401
ever going to go and do
because they never tried it.

00:11:07.401 --> 00:11:09.641
Now, they're stuck doing some menial job

00:11:09.641 --> 00:11:11.478
that they don't like.

00:11:11.478 --> 00:11:13.054
They hate going to work every day,

00:11:13.054 --> 00:11:14.667
where they have something inside

00:11:14.667 --> 00:11:18.005
that could really contribute
to the world and not doing it.

00:11:18.005 --> 00:11:21.191
I don't know, what's your favorite sport?

00:11:21.191 --> 00:11:24.146
- So, watch football, play basketball.

00:11:24.146 --> 00:11:27.225
- So, football, who's the
greatest quarterback of all time?

00:11:27.225 --> 00:11:28.058
- It's got to be Brady now.

00:11:28.058 --> 00:11:30.200
I mean, you can't, you
can't argue with that now.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:31.866
It's just.
- It's timely, right?

00:11:31.866 --> 00:11:32.866
It's timely.

00:11:33.838 --> 00:11:35.262
- Prior to that, it was Montana.

00:11:35.262 --> 00:11:37.424
I would have said Montana,
but it's got to be Brady now.

00:11:37.424 --> 00:11:39.740
- Whether you say Brady,
whether you say Montana,

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:41.986
whether, like, you know, Troy Aikman

00:11:41.986 --> 00:11:44.111
or you know, whatever your hometown hero,

00:11:44.111 --> 00:11:46.410
I would say it's none of those guys.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.639
I would say the greatest
quarterback of all time

00:11:49.639 --> 00:11:51.806
is a manager at McDonald's

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.809
because he never actually
picked up a football

00:11:56.809 --> 00:12:01.067
and he didn't know that he
had that genius level talent.

00:12:01.067 --> 00:12:02.962
And so, you've got Brady,

00:12:02.962 --> 00:12:05.411
Brady is maybe number 100 on the list.

00:12:05.411 --> 00:12:07.752
There's a ton of guys who
would have more potential,

00:12:07.752 --> 00:12:09.849
but they never picked up a football.

00:12:09.849 --> 00:12:11.816
And that's what upsets me.

00:12:11.816 --> 00:12:15.665
People have a lot of this
untapped genius inside of them,

00:12:15.665 --> 00:12:17.233
and instead of going
off and doing the thing

00:12:17.233 --> 00:12:18.566
that they could have,

00:12:18.566 --> 00:12:21.333
Tom Brady-like genius level talent at,

00:12:21.333 --> 00:12:23.320
they're stuck in a crappy
job that they hate,

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:24.828
that pays the bills

00:12:24.828 --> 00:12:26.949
and they look forward to
the weekends and evenings.

00:12:26.949 --> 00:12:29.534
And so, if everybody, if a billion people

00:12:29.534 --> 00:12:32.033
were off doing their thing and showcasing,

00:12:32.033 --> 00:12:34.525
like, imagine a billion
people being Tom Brady

00:12:34.525 --> 00:12:37.241
at what they're doing,
that's an amazing world

00:12:37.241 --> 00:12:38.878
that I want to be a part of.

00:12:38.878 --> 00:12:41.708
- Right, and I always think about that

00:12:41.708 --> 00:12:44.442
as far as what stops us from
actually pursuing those things.

00:12:44.442 --> 00:12:46.132
And if you think about it
in the grand scheme of life,

00:12:46.132 --> 00:12:47.383
looking back at it, it's like,

00:12:47.383 --> 00:12:49.376
my nightmare is that I didn't pursue that,

00:12:49.376 --> 00:12:51.948
not that I didn't accomplish
this, that, or whatever,

00:12:51.948 --> 00:12:54.404
but that I didn't actually
pursue my dreams to the fullest.

00:12:54.404 --> 00:12:55.492
That's the only way you lose.

00:12:55.492 --> 00:12:58.106
That's the only way you fail in my mind.

00:12:58.106 --> 00:12:59.049
- Yeah, I think what stops people

00:12:59.049 --> 00:13:00.456
is self-awareness and fear.

00:13:00.456 --> 00:13:01.460
- Yep, yeah.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:04.117
- Right? Like I didn't know
that I could be an entrepreneur.

00:13:04.117 --> 00:13:05.684
Like I had all these
tendencies growing up,

00:13:05.684 --> 00:13:07.952
but unless I met those
two people in university,

00:13:07.952 --> 00:13:09.428
I wouldn't have been an entrepreneur.

00:13:09.428 --> 00:13:11.100
I would have gone down the banking path.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:12.515
And so, the self-awareness is important.

00:13:12.515 --> 00:13:15.365
And then, the fear to actually
go out and do something,

00:13:15.365 --> 00:13:17.498
not just take that idea
you have and sit on it

00:13:17.498 --> 00:13:18.832
but to take action,

00:13:18.832 --> 00:13:22.033
and one of the ways to combat
the fear of taking action

00:13:22.033 --> 00:13:24.963
is the greater fear of regret.

00:13:24.963 --> 00:13:28.245
It's the Jeff Bezos regret
minimization framework

00:13:28.245 --> 00:13:31.274
that he calls it, where
you're like 100 years old

00:13:31.274 --> 00:13:32.200
looking back on your life.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:33.400
Are you going to regret doing this?

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.404
And if you are, then you got
to make an important decision.

00:13:36.404 --> 00:13:37.765
And that can give you the courage

00:13:37.765 --> 00:13:39.776
to break through whatever
short-term fear you have.

00:13:39.776 --> 00:13:42.510
- Right, right. Well, you
act on it, and that's great.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:46.637
So let's talk then about
so far in your career

00:13:46.637 --> 00:13:49.441
as an enterpreneur, what
has been your most proud

00:13:49.441 --> 00:13:51.108
or rewarding moment?

00:13:54.375 --> 00:13:56.042
- That's a hard one.

00:13:58.351 --> 00:14:01.268
I don't celebrate it almost at all.

00:14:03.375 --> 00:14:05.092
- You and me have that in common.

00:14:05.092 --> 00:14:07.513
- Yeah, honestly I think,
I think high performers

00:14:07.513 --> 00:14:09.281
don't celebrate that much.

00:14:09.281 --> 00:14:11.511
You look at Tom Brady, you say

00:14:11.511 --> 00:14:13.087
greatest quarterback of all time, great.

00:14:13.087 --> 00:14:14.633
Won five Super Bowls, okay.

00:14:14.633 --> 00:14:17.852
I'm sure he's celebrating
day of the parade,

00:14:17.852 --> 00:14:19.962
but what's he focused on now?
- He's already on to the next.

00:14:19.962 --> 00:14:23.053
- It's like 2018 Super
Bowl, let's go guys.

00:14:23.053 --> 00:14:24.549
We're like five weeks behind.

00:14:24.549 --> 00:14:26.882
Let's start training, right?

00:14:28.058 --> 00:14:30.403
So I have a tough time with that question

00:14:30.403 --> 00:14:32.022
because I don't, I don't know.

00:14:32.022 --> 00:14:34.173
I'm proud of a lot of stuff,

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:36.774
but I don't really look back that much.

00:14:36.774 --> 00:14:38.083
It's like, okay, let's keep going.

00:14:38.083 --> 00:14:39.598
Let's have a big impact.

00:14:39.598 --> 00:14:40.701
Let's keep getting better.

00:14:40.701 --> 00:14:42.097
- Okay, let me shift the question then.

00:14:42.097 --> 00:14:45.300
What has been the most powerful message

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.675
that you've gotten from
someone in your audience

00:14:47.675 --> 00:14:49.185
or a follower or somebody
that you've worked with

00:14:49.185 --> 00:14:50.526
that they've really demonstrated,

00:14:50.526 --> 00:14:52.831
man, you've done this for my life?

00:14:52.831 --> 00:14:54.310
Is that a bit easier?

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:55.560
- Yeah. I mean,

00:14:59.038 --> 00:15:00.440
here's one.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.030
'Cause I get, I mean, I'm fortunate,

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:03.305
I'm sure you're at this spot now too,

00:15:03.305 --> 00:15:04.724
where you get a lot, like daily.

00:15:04.724 --> 00:15:07.289
It's amazing. You get a
couple hundred comments a day.

00:15:07.289 --> 00:15:10.636
And a lot of them are
insane life-changing stuff.

00:15:10.636 --> 00:15:14.467
I remember one though in particular.

00:15:14.467 --> 00:15:16.833
It was on a video we did, I
think it was Magic Johnson,

00:15:16.833 --> 00:15:18.865
the basketball player

00:15:18.865 --> 00:15:21.503
and entrepreneur now as well.

00:15:21.503 --> 00:15:24.207
And there was a guy who watched the video

00:15:24.207 --> 00:15:27.040
and he left a comment and he said,

00:15:27.909 --> 00:15:29.782
"My parents taught me growing up

00:15:29.782 --> 00:15:32.949
"that black people were really stupid.

00:15:33.841 --> 00:15:37.636
"And this video just totally
changed my perspective."

00:15:37.636 --> 00:15:39.179
It's like, that's nuts, right,

00:15:39.179 --> 00:15:42.027
that something that I'm involved with

00:15:42.027 --> 00:15:46.194
is changing people's perspective
on things like racism

00:15:47.626 --> 00:15:50.865
and opening their minds to
different points of views,

00:15:50.865 --> 00:15:52.101
where you're not just insulated

00:15:52.101 --> 00:15:54.094
by your immediate
environment of your parents

00:15:54.094 --> 00:15:55.349
and family and community

00:15:55.349 --> 00:15:57.656
'cause if you grow up
in a crappy environment,

00:15:57.656 --> 00:16:00.701
chances are you're going
to be exactly like them.

00:16:00.701 --> 00:16:02.315
And listening to The Entrepreneur Hour

00:16:02.315 --> 00:16:04.932
or watching videos and reading biographies

00:16:04.932 --> 00:16:08.642
and surrounding yourself
with better knowledge

00:16:08.642 --> 00:16:11.585
can help you break free,
broaden your perspective,

00:16:11.585 --> 00:16:13.766
and give you the chance to go
out and do something amazing.

00:16:13.766 --> 00:16:16.604
And so, that one in particular
stands out as that's,

00:16:16.604 --> 00:16:18.946
I mean, that sucks that
they grew up in that.

00:16:18.946 --> 00:16:22.308
But how amazing is that
that something I made

00:16:22.308 --> 00:16:24.119
totally changed his perspective?

00:16:24.119 --> 00:16:25.251
- Yeah, I wonder how old he was,

00:16:25.251 --> 00:16:28.211
how long he's been operating
with that limited belief.

00:16:28.211 --> 00:16:32.564
- Yeah, and then would
continue it on to his kids.

00:16:32.564 --> 00:16:34.976
- No, and yeah, for
generations, absolutely.

00:16:34.976 --> 00:16:37.990
That's crazy. That's awesome though.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:39.466
Alright, so let's shift gears.

00:16:39.466 --> 00:16:41.447
Let's talk about where do you take

00:16:41.447 --> 00:16:43.928
your efforts from here.

00:16:43.928 --> 00:16:45.261
Reaching a billion people, like I said,

00:16:45.261 --> 00:16:46.523
that's a pretty lofty goal,

00:16:46.523 --> 00:16:48.099
albeit it looks like
you're well on your way,

00:16:48.099 --> 00:16:49.727
but what does that look like?

00:16:49.727 --> 00:16:51.425
What does the future look
like for Evan Carmichael

00:16:51.425 --> 00:16:52.258
and what you're going to be doing?

00:16:52.258 --> 00:16:53.091
Is it books?

00:16:53.091 --> 00:16:54.934
Is it continuing to build
your video presence?

00:16:54.934 --> 00:16:56.539
What is that going to
look like for your entail?

00:16:56.539 --> 00:16:58.125
- Yeah, so the billion,

00:16:58.125 --> 00:17:00.264
the billion question gets asked a lot.

00:17:00.264 --> 00:17:03.747
I think for me, it's not so
much about how do I track,

00:17:03.747 --> 00:17:05.020
how do I hit a billion.

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:06.788
It's more that

00:17:06.788 --> 00:17:09.740
the work that I do has
to have the potential

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:11.404
to make a big impact.

00:17:11.404 --> 00:17:13.611
And so, I don't do one-on-one coaching.

00:17:13.611 --> 00:17:15.319
I don't have a hey hire me for an hour

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:18.636
and it costs x amount of dollars.

00:17:18.636 --> 00:17:20.825
The work that I do has to be at scale

00:17:20.825 --> 00:17:22.567
for me to achieve what I want to achieve.

00:17:22.567 --> 00:17:25.488
So, the YouTube channel lets
me have a wide audience.

00:17:25.488 --> 00:17:28.304
Doing The Entrepreneur Hour
lets me reach some more people.

00:17:28.304 --> 00:17:30.620
Writing a book, like I don't
want to self-publish a book.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:32.415
I can make more money
self-publishing a book.

00:17:32.415 --> 00:17:33.895
I went with Penguin Random House

00:17:33.895 --> 00:17:37.245
because that lets me
reach a wider audience.

00:17:37.245 --> 00:17:40.841
And so, I think when you see
really successful people,

00:17:40.841 --> 00:17:43.341
they are really stubborn about

00:17:45.093 --> 00:17:47.284
the mission, the vision, the why,

00:17:47.284 --> 00:17:48.929
like, this is what we're here to do.

00:17:48.929 --> 00:17:50.953
I am here to help a billion entrepreneurs.

00:17:50.953 --> 00:17:52.466
This is what I'm super passionate about

00:17:52.466 --> 00:17:54.808
and wake up every day chasing.

00:17:54.808 --> 00:17:57.612
The how and the execution changes.

00:17:57.612 --> 00:17:59.484
I'm not attached to the how.

00:17:59.484 --> 00:18:01.533
It's like stubbornness to the cause,

00:18:01.533 --> 00:18:04.032
but extreme humility,
like I'll do anything

00:18:04.032 --> 00:18:05.433
to move that thing forward.

00:18:05.433 --> 00:18:08.237
If you told me that cleaning out toilets

00:18:08.237 --> 00:18:10.649
would help me reach a
billion entrepreneurs,

00:18:10.649 --> 00:18:11.806
I would do it.
- Yeah, you'd do it.

00:18:11.806 --> 00:18:13.423
- Right? Like, I'm not above that.

00:18:13.423 --> 00:18:14.340
I'll do it.

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:17.070
And so many people are like,
I'm not going to do that.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.986
That's beneath me, right?

00:18:18.986 --> 00:18:21.859
And most successful leaders,
nothing is beneath them

00:18:21.859 --> 00:18:25.156
to help them accomplish that big goal.

00:18:25.156 --> 00:18:26.411
And so, I can tell you where I'm going

00:18:26.411 --> 00:18:27.911
in the next couple months,

00:18:27.911 --> 00:18:29.121
but where am I going to be in five years?

00:18:29.121 --> 00:18:31.739
I didn't know I was going
to write a book a year ago.

00:18:31.739 --> 00:18:34.233
Five years ago, I would never
say that YouTube would be

00:18:34.233 --> 00:18:36.540
where I'm spending most of my time.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:37.687
So in the next couple months,

00:18:37.687 --> 00:18:39.318
it's hey, I'm promoting the book,

00:18:39.318 --> 00:18:41.265
I'm getting the YouTube channel going.

00:18:41.265 --> 00:18:43.463
It's where I'm spending most of my time

00:18:43.463 --> 00:18:46.257
and what I'm in the short to
mid-term super committed to.

00:18:46.257 --> 00:18:49.050
In a year, in two years, in five years,

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.935
I don't know, but it's going to be

00:18:51.935 --> 00:18:54.650
impacting entrepreneurs in a big way,

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.998
whatever that looks like.

00:18:55.998 --> 00:18:57.368
- Total scalability.

00:18:57.368 --> 00:19:00.465
Alright, so you talked about a guy that's

00:19:00.465 --> 00:19:02.245
he's a manager at McDonald's

00:19:02.245 --> 00:19:04.036
and he could potentially have been

00:19:04.036 --> 00:19:05.736
the Tom Brady or even
better than Tom Brady,

00:19:05.736 --> 00:19:08.637
make Tom Brady look like an afterthought.

00:19:08.637 --> 00:19:12.117
Talk to that guy right
now or that gal right now,

00:19:12.117 --> 00:19:13.701
and they're on the fence about,

00:19:13.701 --> 00:19:15.547
whether it's entrepreneurship
or pursuing whatever it is

00:19:15.547 --> 00:19:17.151
to pursue, what would
you say to that person

00:19:17.151 --> 00:19:19.318
to get them to finally
take that leap of faith

00:19:19.318 --> 00:19:21.818
or hop over that fence?

00:19:21.818 --> 00:19:23.656
- I think it's first
helping them understand

00:19:23.656 --> 00:19:25.843
what it is that they want to do.

00:19:25.843 --> 00:19:29.113
Does the guy even know
that he can play football?

00:19:29.113 --> 00:19:31.134
Has he had any exposure to it?

00:19:31.134 --> 00:19:32.939
Do you want to go out
and start a business?

00:19:32.939 --> 00:19:33.911
What is it in your head?

00:19:33.911 --> 00:19:37.032
Are you happy with your life?

00:19:37.032 --> 00:19:38.364
Are you happy with where
you're at right now?

00:19:38.364 --> 00:19:40.968
You ask most people that,
they're going to say no.

00:19:40.968 --> 00:19:42.422
If they're honest with themselves,

00:19:42.422 --> 00:19:43.646
like if it's a total
stranger, he'd be like,

00:19:43.646 --> 00:19:44.714
yeah, I'm happy,

00:19:44.714 --> 00:19:48.509
but deep down, most people
are not happy with their life.

00:19:48.509 --> 00:19:53.219
So, just getting into that
uncomfortable position

00:19:53.219 --> 00:19:54.682
where you are analyzing,

00:19:54.682 --> 00:19:57.094
'cause instead of actually
trying to fix something,

00:19:57.094 --> 00:19:58.286
we escape.

00:19:58.286 --> 00:19:59.433
- Exactly.

00:19:59.433 --> 00:20:01.462
- Right? It's like.
- No, totally, and definitely.

00:20:01.462 --> 00:20:03.845
- I don't want to think about my problem,

00:20:03.845 --> 00:20:07.146
so let's go watch Big Brother 18.

00:20:07.146 --> 00:20:08.926
And so, just even having the awareness

00:20:08.926 --> 00:20:10.581
to have a conversation around,

00:20:10.581 --> 00:20:12.609
listen, your life is broken,

00:20:12.609 --> 00:20:14.559
you're not happy, you could be doing more,

00:20:14.559 --> 00:20:16.807
you know you could be doing more,

00:20:16.807 --> 00:20:18.706
let's start figuring that out.

00:20:18.706 --> 00:20:20.378
What is that big dream
that maybe you could

00:20:20.378 --> 00:20:22.232
start building towards?

00:20:22.232 --> 00:20:24.328
And then giving them
the support and belief

00:20:24.328 --> 00:20:26.177
and environment to do it.

00:20:26.177 --> 00:20:28.783
What often ends up happening is

00:20:28.783 --> 00:20:30.866
I would be a blip, right?

00:20:32.509 --> 00:20:34.478
Listening to one episode
of The Entrepreneur Hour

00:20:34.478 --> 00:20:35.811
would be a blip.

00:20:37.709 --> 00:20:42.177
And maybe that's enough
of a blip to spark a fire.

00:20:42.177 --> 00:20:44.778
They're just so pent up and
so ready to make a change

00:20:44.778 --> 00:20:47.410
that that one episode, that half an hour,

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.861
makes them want to make a change.

00:20:49.861 --> 00:20:52.814
But for most people, it's just a blip.

00:20:52.814 --> 00:20:54.228
And then, they fall back

00:20:54.228 --> 00:20:56.640
